module lib/hf/jk_direct
  Direct J and K: the members of the J/K stage that recompute the electron
  repulsion integrals from the basis each time instead of reading stored
  ones. The production member screens shell quartets by the Schwarz bound
  and by the density; the second screens by the Schwarz bound only and
  checks the first.

int_jk_is_direct  @c3b8369d
  why   A direct run hands the SCF the basis blob instead of packed
        integrals, whichever direct member it picks; the pipeline asks this
        in one place.
  k     the J/K member number

int_basis_blob  @c629c54a
  why   The SCF drivers pass one integral array to every J/K member. The direct
        members need the whole basis instead, so the basis travels as one flat
        array in that slot. The Schwarz factors of the shell pairs depend on
        the basis only, so they are computed here, once per run, instead of
        at every J/K build.
  atoms      the atoms table
  shells     the shells table
  prims      the primitives table
  M          the Cartesian-to-spherical matrix, `[nao_cart, nao_sph]`; read only for a spherical basis
  spherical  `1` for a spherical basis, `0` for Cartesian
  natm       how many atoms
  nsh        how many shells
  npr        how many primitives
  atw        the atoms table's row width
  shw        the shells table's row width
  prw        the primitives table's row width
  nc         how many Cartesian basis functions
  ns         how many spherical basis functions, `0` for a Cartesian basis
  sph        `1` for a spherical basis, so the Schwarz factors are those of spherical quartets
  Q          the Schwarz factor of each pair of the store
  npair      how many pairs
  b          the blob: the counts and the row widths, then atoms, shells, primitives, M and Q
  k          the next free position in b

  = ns#2  a spherical basis also carries M, so its size goes into the header
  = Q     the Schwarz factors, computed once with the blob
  = b     room for the header and every table
  = k     the tables start after the header
  = b#11  the atoms table, row by row; shells, primitives and M follow the same way, then Q

int_blob_pack  @60676db1
  why   int_blob_basis and the fuzz tests read the atoms, shells, primitives
        and M back out of a blob with it.
  b     the blob
  k0    where the table starts in it
  rows  the table's rows
  cols  the table's columns
  P     the table
  k     the next position to read
  r     the row being read
  c     the column being read

int_blob_basis  @3fee6987
  why   Both direct members read the same tables out of the blob; the record
        names them.
  b       the blob
  natm    how many atoms
  nsh     how many shells
  npr     how many primitives
  nc      how many Cartesian functions
  ns      how many spherical functions; `0` means the basis is Cartesian
  npair   how many shell pairs Q covers
  k       the position in the blob of the next table
  atoms   the atoms table
  shells  the shells table
  prims   the primitives table
  sph     `1` for a spherical basis
  M       the Cartesian-to-spherical matrix, `[nc, ns]`, or a placeholder

int_dens_cart  @0b6db683
  why   The integrals are computed over Cartesian functions, so a spherical
        density is moved there first: `Tr(D M^T X M) = Tr((M D M^T) X)`.
  B     the basis read from the blob
  D     the densities, `[nd, ns, ns]`
  nd    how many densities
  nc    how many Cartesian functions
  Dc    the densities over Cartesian functions, `M D M^T`
  d     a density

int_jk_run  @5b6946ee
  why   J and K built over Cartesian functions go back to the run's
        functions, `M^T J M`.
  B     the basis read from the blob
  R     J and K over Cartesian functions, `[2 nd, nc, nc]`
  ns    how many spherical functions
  out   J and K in spherical functions, `[2 nd, ns, ns]`
  r     a J or K matrix

int_jk_direct  @ef1933d1
  why   It lets the SCF run without storing any ERIs: J and K come from
        integrals recomputed each iteration on the generated kernels
        (lib/hf/mdg_eri), in the run's functions, screened by
        the density as PySCF's direct SCF screens, and the member fits the
        same registry as the in-core ones, so the same loop and the same
        cross-check serve both
        [test "direct J/K agrees with the in-core members, Cartesian and spherical"].
  X1    not read
  X2    the basis blob of int_basis_blob
  X3    not read
  D     the densities, `[nd, ns, ns]` in the run's basis functions
  opts  the run's options: the Schwarz and the density thresholds
  B     the basis read from the blob

int_jk_direct_full  @e2125d5f
  why   The direct build that skips only what the Schwarz bound allows, the
        path the production member replaced; the registry runs it beside
        the others on small cases.
  X1    not read
  X2    the basis blob of int_basis_blob
  X3    not read
  D     the densities, `[nd, ns, ns]` in the run's basis functions
  opts  the run's options: the Schwarz threshold
  B     the basis read from the blob
